WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:05.040
Film Courage: So knowing that you’d rather
critique or analyze story rather than sit

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:07.009
down and write it out…

00:00:07.009 --> 00:00:09.680
Peter Russell, screenwriter/script doctor:
Okay…stop…I don’t anymore because I

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:11.940
get paid so much more for actually writing.

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:13.880
Film Courage: Well how did this happen?

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:18.560
Peter: I guess because the things that I’ve
been analyzing and thinking about and teaching

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:23.410
for twenty years, a lot of them have turned
out to actually work.

00:00:23.410 --> 00:00:25.880
So it’s kind of nice.

00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:28.600
I’ve been talking about the BMOC [Beginning,
middle, obstacle and climax], the structural

00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:30.730
tool and the core wound and everything else.

00:00:30.730 --> 00:00:35.199
But what it turns out is that these things
actually do help and even though it helped

00:00:35.199 --> 00:00:41.329
other writers and I’ve been told that for
a long time, it’s great and a relief that

00:00:41.329 --> 00:00:42.330
they actually work.

00:00:42.330 --> 00:00:53.340
So what I find as a writer as opposed to a
teacher is that these tools work so it’s

00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:55.269
a relief to have them.

00:00:55.269 --> 00:01:01.319
So when I’m stymied or I can’t move forward,
I go back to my own work, I go back to my

00:01:01.319 --> 00:01:05.950
own book, I go back to my own lectures and
I go “Where am I?”

00:01:05.950 --> 00:01:12.090
I’m working on a miniseries now about Jack
Johnson, the first African American Heavy

00:01:12.090 --> 00:01:15.150
Weight champion in the U.S.

00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:21.719
And it’s been sold to production companies
but they want changes and I’m co-writing

00:01:21.719 --> 00:01:30.150
with a brilliant guy that I met actually at
a talk I gave and I often meet people that

00:01:30.150 --> 00:01:36.189
I collaborate with and he had this project
and it was marvelous.

00:01:36.189 --> 00:01:43.799
But one of the things I talk about is the
superhero model because superheroes are big

00:01:43.799 --> 00:01:54.770
now whether they are serious like some of
the serious stories or if they are a comic

00:01:54.770 --> 00:01:57.350
like in DEADPOOL 2 (we can talk about that
too).

00:01:57.350 --> 00:02:07.490
But the superhero is huge now and so the superhero
model involves a very particular tool we can

00:02:07.490 --> 00:02:08.720
talk about at some point.

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:09.890
I don’t know if you want to talk about it
now?

00:02:09.890 --> 00:02:16.370
But what I decided was because several people
had taken a pass at Jack Johnson and I think

00:02:16.370 --> 00:02:22.290
someone is actually making a movie but not
very big and I don’t think I am giving away

00:02:22.290 --> 00:02:27.190
anything secrets wise.

00:02:27.190 --> 00:02:33.989
The reason a superhero is fun too watch is
not for the same reason most heroes are fun

00:02:33.989 --> 00:02:35.040
to watch.

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:40.299
Most heroes are fun to watch because they
are wounded and they’ve got some big bleeding

00:02:40.299 --> 00:02:44.720
wound and we’re going to watch them heal
it.

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:47.420
We’re going to watch them (that’s the
story).

00:02:47.420 --> 00:02:49.680
Superheroes, no.

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:56.680
Superheroes are not wounded, they are superheroes
(the old-fashioned superhero).

00:02:56.680 --> 00:03:01.450
In 300 Zack Snyder’s he doesn’t have a
wound, it’s not like “My mommy hurt me

00:03:01.450 --> 00:03:03.879
and I’ve got to figure out how to do that.”

00:03:03.879 --> 00:03:04.890
It’s like no.

00:03:04.890 --> 00:03:11.610
In 300 he’s an old-fashioned superhero because
all the reason we want to watch old-fashioned

00:03:11.610 --> 00:03:16.750
superheroes is to see if he can lift the bigger
weight that the villain is putting on him.

00:03:16.750 --> 00:03:21.790
The four crucial points of the story, the
villain just gets bigger and says “Hey,

00:03:21.790 --> 00:03:24.530
you can lift that 300 pounds, huh?”

00:03:24.530 --> 00:03:28.959
Think as the villain and the superhero as
a deranged trainer at Gold’s Gym and he’s

00:03:28.959 --> 00:03:32.469
like “Alright, so you’re going to lift
300 pounds…Arrggh!

00:03:32.469 --> 00:03:33.469
Okay.”

00:03:33.469 --> 00:03:35.970
So at the 30-page point you lifted the 300
pounds.

00:03:35.970 --> 00:03:39.159
Okay so at the 60-page point we’re going
to put 900 pounds on there.

00:03:39.159 --> 00:03:40.579
Can you lift that?”

00:03:40.579 --> 00:03:46.690
So the secret of the superhero story, that
kind of superhero story (there are several

00:03:46.690 --> 00:03:54.030
kinds) is that can he lift the bigger weight
the villain has put on that rack.

00:03:54.030 --> 00:04:01.939
So that was our job with Jack Johnson, how
can the villain get bigger at the end of each

00:04:01.939 --> 00:04:02.939
episode?

00:04:02.939 --> 00:04:05.090
What’s the bigger weight they can put on
Jack Johnson?

00:04:05.090 --> 00:04:09.989
Now instead of figuring out weeks and months
figuring out how is he wounded?

00:04:09.989 --> 00:04:13.379
How are we going to give him mentors on one
side, wounders on the other.

00:04:13.379 --> 00:04:14.379
No!

00:04:14.379 --> 00:04:15.379
It’s all about, how big is that villain?

00:04:15.379 --> 00:04:17.550
Is that villain getting bigger?

00:04:17.550 --> 00:04:21.170
Because if the villain is getting bigger,
the audience is going to watch.

00:04:21.170 --> 00:04:23.330
Shakespeare did the same thing by the way.

00:04:23.330 --> 00:04:28.670
And I used to direct Shakespeare in New York
after school.

00:04:28.670 --> 00:04:32.441
And Shakespeare had wrestling matches in which
the villain just got bigger and bigger and

00:04:32.441 --> 00:04:40.510
so in other words long story short (not any
more but) I really can use my tool to solve

00:04:40.510 --> 00:04:44.420
story problems that I’m getting paid to
solve.

00:04:44.420 --> 00:04:49.450
So when they work, I’m so excited, I’m
so ecstatic.

00:04:49.450 --> 00:04:56.450
It means all the 20 years I’ve spent doing
this is actually paying off for me and also

00:04:56.450 --> 00:05:01.110
now I have a little more credibility in when
I teach, right?

00:05:01.110 --> 00:05:04.700
Because the wrap on teachers is “Ahh…well
you can teach but you can’t do.”

00:05:04.700 --> 00:05:05.700
Right?

00:05:05.700 --> 00:05:08.570
“You’re teaching, you’re not doing,
okay I get it.”

00:05:08.570 --> 00:05:09.920
Well now I’m doing.

00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:15.280
So I can say “Okay these tools actually
do work, so shut up.

00:05:15.280 --> 00:05:16.530
Shut up!

00:05:16.530 --> 00:05:18.680
I did it!

00:05:18.680 --> 00:05:19.610
Nah…nah…nah.”

